--TEST--
`?->` is a read operator only: a nullsafe property, and a nullsafe subscript over one, are both refused as assignment targets — `rule:types/grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `?->` answers `null` when the receiver is `null`, and `null` is not a place
// to write to, so an assignment through one would silently do nothing on that
// path. PHP refuses the same two spellings ("Can't use nullsafe operator in
// write context"), and so does the plain-value form and the element form
// alike — the subscript chain is walked down to its root, which is where the
// `?->` sits.
class Plain {
    public int $n;
    public array<string> $rows;

    public function constructor() {
        $this->n = 0;
        array<string> $seed = [];
        $this->rows = $seed;
    }
}

?Plain $maybe = new Plain();
$maybe?->n = 1;
$maybe?->rows["0"] = "z";
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0479]: `?->` cannot be written through
   --> case.nvs:20:1
%A
error[E0479]: `?->` cannot be written through
   --> case.nvs:21:1
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
